</div></div>

<article class="art" id="a1">
<header><h3>1. Agile Team Charter</h3><span class="meta">Shared team purpose and working agreements</span></header>
<div class="two">
<div class="ev"><h4 class="lab">Evidence</h4>
<ul><li>Project vision and the four problems CareConnect solves</li><li>Team roles, values and Scrum events</li><li>Working agreements: branching, pull request reviews, communication</li><li>Definition of Ready and Definition of Done</li><li>Risks, conflict resolution and sign-off</li></ul>
<a href="https://github.com/NuyraSwanson/careconnect-agile-portfolio/blob/main/01-agile-team-charter.md">Read the full charter</a></div>
<div class="rf"><h4 class="lab">Reflection (STAR)</h4><dl>
<dt>Situation</dt><dd>At the start of the placement project our team needed a shared way of working before writing any code.</dd>
<dt>Task</dt><dd>I helped define our roles, values and rules so everyone knew what was expected.</dd>
<dt>Action</dt><dd>I contributed to the working agreements, such as requiring a reviewer on every pull request, and to the Definition of Done, which included checking role-based access before a story could close.</dd>
<dt>Result</dt><dd>I learned that a charter prevents friction before it starts. It gave us a fair reference point when workloads or opinions differed. [Edit: add one real example from your team.]</dd></dl></div>
</div></article>

<article class="art" id="a2">
<header><h3>2. User Stories and Tasks</h3><span class="meta">What each user needs, broken into work</span></header>
<div class="two">
<div class="ev"><h4 class="lab">Evidence</h4>
<ul><li>14 stories across six epics: accounts, booking, live queue, SOS, records and platform</li><li>Four personas: patient, doctor or nurse, admin and responder</li><li>Acceptance criteria written for every story</li><li>Tasks with type and hour estimates</li></ul>
<a href="https://github.com/NuyraSwanson/careconnect-agile-portfolio/blob/main/02-user-stories-and-tasks.md">Read the user stories</a></div>
<div class="rf"><h4 class="lab">Reflection (STAR)</h4><dl>
<dt>Situation</dt><dd>CareConnect serves very different users, from patients in a hurry to staff managing a queue.</dd>
<dt>Task</dt><dd>Turn their needs into stories the team could build and test.</dd>
<dt>Action</dt><dd>I wrote stories in the &ldquo;As a, I want, so that&rdquo; format with testable acceptance criteria, then split large stories such as booking into smaller tasks.</dd>
<dt>Result</dt><dd>Writing from the user&rsquo;s point of view made me think about why a feature mattered, and clear criteria made testing far easier. [Edit: add a real story that changed after feedback.]</dd></dl></div>
</div></article>

<article class="art" id="a3">
<header><h3>3. Product Backlog</h3><span class="meta">Everything the product needs, in priority order</span></header>
<div class="two">
<div class="ev"><h4 class="lab">Evidence</h4>
<ul><li>Ordered backlog with MoSCoW priority, story points, sprint and status</li><li>Future enhancements: push notifications, offline support, USSD booking, multilingual support</li><li>Technical items such as a full RLS audit and automated tests</li><li>Written prioritisation rationale</li></ul>
<a href="https://github.com/NuyraSwanson/careconnect-agile-portfolio/blob/main/03-product-backlog.md">Read the product backlog</a></div>
<div class="rf"><h4 class="lab">Reflection (STAR)</h4><dl>
<dt>Situation</dt><dd>We had more ideas than time, so we had to decide what to build first.</dd>
<dt>Task</dt><dd>Prioritise the work so the most valuable and riskiest items came first.</dd>
<dt>Action</dt><dd>We ranked items with MoSCoW, put security and authentication before any feature storing health data, and parked ideas like the AI assistant for later.</dd>
<dt>Result</dt><dd>I learned that a backlog is a living list that I should keep refining, and that saying &ldquo;not now&rdquo; protects the quality of what ships. [Edit: add what you reprioritised and why.]</dd></dl></div>
</div></article>

<article class="art" id="a4">
<header><h3>4. Sprint Backlog</h3><span class="meta">The plan for one sprint</span></header>
<div class="two">
<div class="ev"><h4 class="lab">Evidence</h4>
<ul><li>Sprint goal: deliver the live queue and a location-based SOS</li><li>Committed stories with points and owners</li><li>Task board with estimates against actual hours</li><li>Burndown, impediments, Definition of Done check, review and retrospective</li></ul>
<a href="https://github.com/NuyraSwanson/careconnect-agile-portfolio/blob/main/04-sprint-backlog.md">Read the sprint backlog</a></div>
<div class="rf"><h4 class="lab">Reflection (STAR)</h4><dl>
<dt>Situation</dt><dd>Real-time features were the hardest part of CareConnect, and we hit sync and permission problems.</dd>
<dt>Task</dt><dd>Commit to a realistic amount of work and keep the board honest.</dd>
<dt>Action</dt><dd>We recorded blockers such as Realtime events not reaching every device, and used the burndown and retrospective to adjust.</dd>
<dt>Result</dt><dd>I learned to test on two devices early and to raise blockers the same day. [Edit: use your real sprint numbers.]</dd></dl></div>
</div></article>

<article class="art" id="a5">
<header><h3>5. Agile Logbook</h3><span class="meta">What happened, week by week</span></header>
<div class="two">
<div class="ev"><h4 class="lab">Evidence</h4>
<ul><li>Weekly entries: work done, practices used, blockers and learning</li><li>Ceremony log with dates and outcomes</li><li>Skills before and after the placement</li><li>Final reflection</li></ul>
<a href="https://github.com/NuyraSwanson/careconnect-agile-portfolio/blob/main/05-agile-logbook.md">Read the logbook</a></div>
<div class="rf"><h4 class="lab">Reflection (STAR)</h4><dl>
<dt>Situation</dt><dd>Over the placement my understanding of Agile moved from theory to daily practice.</dd>
<dt>Task</dt><dd>Keep a record that shows what I did and what I learned.</dd>
<dt>Action</dt><dd>I wrote entries during each sprint and linked blockers to the retrospective actions that followed.</dd>
<dt>Result</dt><dd>Looking back, I see that I communicate blockers earlier and design permissions alongside features. [Edit: write this in your own words.]</dd></dl></div>
</div></article>

<div class="box"><h3>How the artefacts connect</h3>
<p>The charter set how we work. User stories described what users need, and the product backlog ordered those stories by value. The sprint backlog turned the top of the backlog into a committed plan, and the logbook recorded what actually happened and what I learned. Together they show one cycle of Agile delivery on CareConnect, from agreement to reflection. [Edit: add your overall learning in your own words.]</p></div>
</div></section>

